# 8. CONTROL FIRESTORE READS AND WRITES

This is a critical requirement.

The entire application shares the Firestore free-plan resource allowance. Coding Lab must not introduce excessive read/write activity.

Avoid inefficient saving patterns.

### A. Local-first editing

While learners work, use IndexedDB for frequent local changes.

Do not send every block movement to Firestore.

Do not write every time:
- A block moves.
- A block is connected.
- A sprite moves.
- A costume is selected.
- The stage changes.
- A cursor moves.
- The editor renders.
- A learner opens a toolbox category.

These are local editing activities, not database events.

### B. Controlled cloud autosave

In Assessment mode:

- Save local changes to IndexedDB first.
- Debounce cloud saving.
- Use approximately 20–30 seconds of inactivity as an initial autosave interval.
- Allow manual Save Progress.
- Avoid multiple simultaneous save requests.
- Coalesce pending changes into one save.
- Do not save if the content hash has not changed.
- Do not repeatedly overwrite unchanged asset manifests.
- Ensure saving is idempotent where possible.

The interval should be configurable centrally.

### C. Save-state feedback

The learner must be able to distinguish:

- Saving locally.
- Saved on this device.
- Uploading assets.
- Saving to the LMS.
- Saved to the LMS.
- Offline or cloud sync pending.
- Save failed.

Never display "Saved to LMS" merely because the project was saved locally.

### D. Firestore query optimisation

- Do not use constant polling.
- Do not attach unnecessary real-time listeners to every project.
- Load only the selected assessment and learner project.
- Use one-time reads where live synchronisation is unnecessary.
- Avoid reloading complete projects when only metadata is required.
- Do not query every learner's project when a tutor opens one submission.
- Retrieve only the data relevant to the selected learner and assessment.
- Paginate project/submission lists.
- Avoid repeated reads of static assessment configuration.
- Cache suitable read-only metadata.
- Avoid duplicate document writes.
- Do not create unlimited draft revisions.

Firestore write batches may be used for atomic consistency, but remember that batching does not make individual document writes free. Optimise the number of documents actually written.

